<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Dampierre</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Dampierre was a French noble family from Dampierre in Champagne. William of Dampierre married Margaret, heiress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, and their son Guy of Dampierre bought Namur in 1263 and became Count of Flanders in 1278. The Flemish line passed by the marriage of Margaret III to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> in 1369, while the Namur line sold its county to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> in 1421 and ended in 1429.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-dampierre.jpg" alt="House of Dampierre" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blason_maison_de_Dampierre_(Champagne).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The independent county ended with the extinction of the male line of the house of <b>Dampierre</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1263 the <b>Dampierre</b> counts of Flanders ruled it, and Namur's citadel grew into a major fortress.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-namur">County of Namur</a></li></ul></section>
